WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.749
Real SQL work is rarely a single SELECT against a single table.

00:00:04.749 --> 00:00:07.498
It is layered: filter, group, rank, compare.

00:00:07.498 --> 00:00:10.248
The five patterns on this page, together, solve about eighty percent of the data questions you will ever be asked.

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:12.997
Each one has a name.

00:00:12.997 --> 00:00:15.746
Funnel.

00:00:15.746 --> 00:00:18.495
Ladder.

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:21.244
Timeline.

00:00:21.244 --> 00:00:23.994
Comparison.

00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:26.743
Exception.

00:00:26.743 --> 00:00:29.492
For each, we will look at the shape, then read the query.

00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:33.114
The funnel: who did step A but not step B?

00:00:33.114 --> 00:00:36.736
Played a song, but never shared one.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:40.359
Each stage keeps fewer, so the picture narrows.

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:43.981
A thousand played, four hundred fifty made a playlist, only eighty-seven shared.

00:00:43.981 --> 00:00:47.000
Now read the query, top to bottom.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.018
SELECT user id and name.

00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:53.037
FROM users.

00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:56.055
WHERE user id is IN the set of people who listened, the players.

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:59.074
AND user id is NOT IN the set who shared.

00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:02.092
Keep the players, subtract the sharers.

00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:05.111
What is left played but never shared.

00:01:05.111 --> 00:01:08.083
The ladder ranks within groups.

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:11.056
Top three songs per genre.

00:01:11.056 --> 00:01:14.028
Top salesperson per region.

00:01:14.028 --> 00:01:17.000
Number every row inside its own group, then keep the top rungs and cut the rest.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.328
Read it inside out.

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:25.656
The inner SELECT computes ROW_NUMBER OVER, PARTITION BY genre, ORDER BY plays descending.

00:01:25.656 --> 00:01:29.985
That numbers each genre's songs, one for the most played, two for the next.

00:01:29.985 --> 00:01:34.313
The outer SELECT then keeps WHERE row-number is three or less.

00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:38.641
Partition, order, number, filter.

00:01:38.641 --> 00:01:41.880
The timeline tracks a value as it accumulates over time.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.120
A running total of listens.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.359
A seven-day moving average.

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:51.598
The line only climbs: three, then eight, then ten, then fourteen.

00:01:51.598 --> 00:01:54.663
The query.

00:01:54.663 --> 00:01:57.728
SELECT date and hours.

00:01:57.728 --> 00:02:00.793
Then SUM of hours OVER an ORDER BY date window.

00:02:00.793 --> 00:02:03.858
That ORDER BY inside the OVER clause is what makes it cumulative: each row sums itself and everything before it.

00:02:03.858 --> 00:02:06.923
FROM daily listening.

00:02:06.923 --> 00:02:09.988
One window, a running total.

00:02:09.988 --> 00:02:13.378
The comparison measures one row against its group's average.

00:02:13.378 --> 00:02:16.768
This artist versus their genre.

00:02:16.768 --> 00:02:20.158
A bar above the dashed line beats its peers; a bar below trails them.

00:02:20.158 --> 00:02:24.263
The query.

00:02:24.263 --> 00:02:28.368
SELECT artist name, genre, plays.

00:02:28.368 --> 00:02:32.474
Then plays minus AVG of plays OVER, PARTITION BY genre.

00:02:32.474 --> 00:02:36.579
The window computes the genre average once and attaches it to every row, so the subtraction gives each artist's distance from the pack.

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:40.684
Positive is above, negative is below.

00:02:40.684 --> 00:02:44.105
The exception finds outliers.

00:02:44.105 --> 00:02:47.526
A daily count that jumped fifty percent overnight.

00:02:47.526 --> 00:02:50.947
Most points sit in a normal band; the spike pops out, flagged.

00:02:50.947 --> 00:02:55.582
The query, in two steps.

00:02:55.582 --> 00:03:00.217
The first common table expression, changes, uses LAG OVER, PARTITION BY song, ORDER BY date, to grab yesterday's listens onto today's row.

00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:04.851
The second computes percent change from previous to current.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:09.486
Then the final SELECT keeps WHERE percent change is over fifty.

00:03:09.486 --> 00:03:14.121
LAG, compute, filter.

00:03:14.121 --> 00:03:19.717
One full walkthrough.

00:03:19.717 --> 00:03:25.313
Find breakout artists with over fifty percent month-over-month growth.

00:03:25.313 --> 00:03:30.909
The query stitches two patterns together: timeline, to compute the previous month with LAG, and comparison, to compute the percent change.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:36.505
Three common table expressions, and every line is a recognizable piece of the patterns we just walked through.

00:03:36.505 --> 00:03:41.277
Writing SQL is problem solving.

00:03:41.277 --> 00:03:46.048
The same five shapes come up over and over: funnel, ladder, timeline, comparison, exception.

00:03:46.048 --> 00:03:50.820
Once you can name the shape and read its query, you have a starting structure to work from.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.592
You map your tables and columns onto it, and adapt.
